This program implements a Standard Variational Autoencoder (S-VAE) designed to model trajectory data (2057 features) for the Minimum Threat Exposure Problem. 
The architecture uses a encoder-decoder structure to compress trajectories into a 32-dimensional latent space and reconstruct them with minimal error. 
Training is performed using a combination of mean squared reconstruction loss and Kullback-Leibler divergence to regularize the latent space. 
The model is trained on 200 trajectories sampled from a dataset of 1000, with early stopping based on total epoch loss. 
After training, the best-performing model is used to generate 1000 synthetic trajectories by sampling from the latent space, which are saved for further analysis or downstream use in planning and control.
